  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/assets/b52bd85700ddc19132ab0cbd0c9243db92bb16145c16bf9bd5b0b0fa56434d9f.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\ge 3$ is shown above. Estimate the area $A$ under the curve over the interval $[4,7]$ using a left Riemann sum with $3$ rectangles of equal width.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A\u2248$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Since $x\\ge 3,$ we have that $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x.$\n\nFirst, let's compute the step size $\u0394x,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our integral are $a=4$ and $b=7,$ and there are $n=3$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\n\u0394x=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{7-4}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/assets/18d2d2d79746d5782d27769b644b31f36c76fc7cf9d867788490d74935439b4e.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA\u2248(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{\u23de}{f(4)+f(5)+f(6)}})\\cdot {{\u0394x}_{\u23df}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $8$ |\n| $5$ | $15$ |\n| $6$ | $24$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & \u2248(8+15+24)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =47\\end{aligned}\n$$",
